{
  "dom_id": "step-q327167",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14224904/assets/a41c728f9a82b563203fa0eb6944617948cf12c048be2553b803b06820d0b896.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(-3,4)$ with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $6,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(-1,4).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$",
  "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-3,4),$ $a=2$ and $b=6.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$",
